<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
      content="default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.github.com">
<title>Calendar</title>
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="icon.svg">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#faf7f2" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#17130f" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

<section id="needs-setup" hidden>
  <header><h1>Calendar</h1><a class="icon-btn" href="../">✕</a></header>
  <p class="lede">Mine is not set up on this device yet.</p>
  <p><a href="../">Open Mine</a> and enter the repo and token once; the calendar then uses them.</p>
</section>

<main id="cal" hidden>
  <div class="topbar">
    <!-- leftmost: the way to the other kind of view, and the zoom -->
    <div class="tb-side">
      <button id="board" class="nav ic" aria-label="Kanban"></button>
      <button id="zoom-btn" class="nav ic" aria-label="Zoom" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false"></button>
    </div>
    <!-- centred: the period. The label opens the picker, and that is all it does. -->
    <div class="tb-mid">
      <button id="prev" class="nav" aria-label="Previous">‹</button>
      <button id="period" class="period" aria-label="Pick a date" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false"><span id="period-label">—</span></button>
      <button id="next" class="nav" aria-label="Next">›</button>
    </div>
    <!-- rightmost: the hamburger, and the way out -->
    <div class="tb-side end">
      <button id="menu-btn" class="nav ic" aria-label="Filter and settings"
              aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false"></button>
      <a class="nav ic" id="home" href="../" aria-label="Back to Mine"></a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="view" id="view"></div>
  <!-- the sync line reports and is pressed: it is what you are looking at when you want to
       know why it says what it says -->
  <button class="sync" id="sync" type="button" title="Open the logs"></button>
</main>

<!-- dropdown date picker: a month at a time, a 9-year grid, or a list of sprints -->
<div class="pop" id="picker" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <button id="pick-title" class="pop-title" type="button"><span id="pick-label">—</span></button>
    <span class="spacer"></span>
    <button id="pick-prev" class="nav" type="button" aria-label="Previous">‹</button>
    <button id="pick-next" class="nav" type="button" aria-label="Next">›</button>
  </div>
  <div class="pick-body" id="pick-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the zoom: day/week/month/year in a calendar view, vertical/horizontal on the board -->
<div class="pop" id="zoom-pop" hidden>
  <div id="zoom-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the day preview, centred like every other overlay -->
<div class="pop centred" id="day-pop" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <strong id="day-title">—</strong><span class="spacer"></span>
    <button class="ib" id="day-add" type="button" title="Add an entry on this day" aria-label="Add an entry on this day"></button>
    <button class="ib" id="day-close" type="button" title="Close" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>
  <div id="day-list" class="day-list"></div>
</div>

<!-- Leaving a popup that holds unsaved changes asks first, once, unless told not to. -->
<div class="modal ask" id="ask" hidden>
  <div class="card">
    <p class="ask-q">Discard the changes?</p>
    <label class="ask-never"><input type="checkbox" id="ask-never"> don't ask again</label>
    <div class="askrow">
      <button type="button" class="ghost" id="ask-keep">keep editing</button>
      <button type="button" class="danger" id="ask-discard">discard</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- One popup for an entry, read and written in the same rows. Every row, the top row and
     the body are built in app code; one disk writes, and it appears only when there is
     something to write. -->
<div class="modal" id="popup" hidden>
  <form class="card" id="entry-form" autocomplete="off">
    <div class="iconbar" id="f-icons"></div>
    <div id="f-rows"></div>
    <p class="msg" id="f-msg"></p>
  </form>
</div>

<!-- A row's own popup, anchored to the row that opened it: the row keeps its icon, its value
     and its trashcan, so nothing moves under the finger that tapped it. -->
<div class="pop fpop" id="fpop" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <strong id="fpop-title">—</strong><span class="spacer"></span>
    <button class="ib" id="fpop-close" type="button"
            title="Close this field" aria-label="Close this field"></button>
  </div>
  <div id="fpop-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the group manager, the one survivor of the sub-popup: reached from the panel, edits no
     field of an entry, and writes groups.md itself. -->
<div class="pop centred sub" id="sub" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <strong id="sub-title">—</strong><span class="spacer"></span>
    <button class="ib" id="sub-close" type="button" title="Close" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>
  <div id="sub-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the hamburger's three errands, named before any is opened -->
<div class="pop" id="menu-pop" hidden>
  <div id="menu-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the logs: what the last sync attempt did, what is still waiting, and the way to retry it or
     give up on it. Centred, like the settings: it is read rather than read past. -->
<div class="centred panel" id="logs-panel" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <h2>Logs</h2><span class="spacer"></span>
    <button class="ib" id="logs-close" type="button" title="Close" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>
  <div id="logs-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the filter: a dropdown off the hamburger, because the view it narrows has to stay in sight -->
<div class="pop" id="filter-pop" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <h2>Filter</h2><span class="spacer"></span>
    <button class="ib" id="filter-close" type="button" title="Close" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>
  <div id="filter-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the categories or the stages, opened off their icon in the filter and drawn over it -->
<div class="pop" id="filter-sub" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <h2 id="filter-sub-title"></h2><span class="spacer"></span>
    <button class="ib" id="filter-sub-close" type="button" title="Close" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>
  <div id="filter-sub-body"></div>
</div>

<!-- the settings: the nine rows that change once, centred like every other overlay -->
<div class="centred panel" id="settings-panel" hidden>
  <div class="pop-head">
    <h2>Settings</h2><span class="spacer"></span>
    <button class="ib" id="settings-close" type="button" title="Close" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>
  <div id="settings-body"></div>
</div>

<div class="scrim" id="scrim" hidden></div>
<div class="scrim sub-scrim" id="sub-scrim" hidden></div>

<script type="module" src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
# Mine

Mine is a set of PWAs (Progressive Web Apps) to digitalize ones personal or professional life.
The key idea is to save everything in a repo as markdown files. This can be calendar entries,
diaries, markdowns extracted from voice recordings / pdfs / .. These .md files can easily be
ported to new apps without a vendor lock-in and are processed via an (AI) assistant to help you
plan your day, keep track of tasks, remind you of memories a.k.a. a second brain.

To keep complexity to a minimum each use-case lives in a separate folder. e.g. the calendar PWA
lives completely in a 'calendar' folder as does the zettelkasten and all the other apps. Each
folder contains a 'sample' folder with samples how the markdowns shall look, a REQUIREMENTS.md
describing how the PWA works and a TECHNICAL-REQUIREMENTS.md describing how it is built.

See [TECHNICAL-REQUIREMENTS.md](TECHNICAL-REQUIREMENTS.md) for how this is built, and
[TEST-REQUIREMENTS.md](TEST-REQUIREMENTS.md) for how it is checked.

## One app, one setup

Mine installs as **one** app on the phone, with one icon. The apps in the subfolders are pages
inside it, not separate installs.

This is what makes the central requirement work: **the GitHub repo and token are entered once,
in the main app, and every app in a subfolder uses them.** Verified on iPhone and Android.

## Main app

A tile per app: its `icon.svg` above its folder name.

Above the tiles, apps may contribute a summary. The calendar's shows today's entries and the
next one — which matters, because opening the app is the only reminder that works (see below).

## First run

1. Open the app URL and add it to the home screen (iOS: Share → Add to Home Screen;
   Android: menu → Install).
2. The setup screen asks for GitHub owner, data repo, branch, and a personal access token.
3. The app tests the connection and reports clearly if it fails.

Settings are stored on the device only. The token is never displayed again after entry, and the
app warns 14 days before it expires.

Two settings belong to Mine rather than to any one app, and the same two rows appear in the main
settings and in each app's: **theme** (system, light or dark) and **date format** (auto, which
follows the device's locale, or `dd.mm.yyyy`, or `mm/dd/yy`). Both default to following the
device, so Mine looks like the rest of the phone until you say otherwise.

## How Mine looks

**Paper, not screen.** The page is a warm off-white rather than white, and the surfaces that sit
above it — a popup, a panel, a card, a tile — are a shade lighter than the page and edged with a
hairline. That is what makes an overlay read as lifted, so none of them needs a heavy border or
a dark drop shadow. There is no neutral grey anywhere in the palette: every colour in it, down
to the rules and the muted text, has warmth in it. The dark theme is the same paper at night —
a warm near-black with brown in it, paper-coloured ink — and not a second palette.

**One accent**, a terracotta, and an app highlights its own thing with it rather than naming a
colour of its own: the calendar's today is that accent. **Serif for what is read, sans for what
is operated** — a heading, a period label, an entry's title and a rendered body's headings are
set in Charter; every control, label and value is set in the system sans.

**Icons are outlines**, drawn on one 16×16 box at a 1.2–1.4 stroke.

One convention is the same in every app: **a save icon is green and a trashcan is red**, wherever
either appears — a trashcan is red even where it only clears one field of one entry, because the
rule is the icon, not the consequence. Both are **glyphs on no
background**: a save is a green disk, a trashcan is a red trashcan. **Nothing in Mine is a filled
green button.** The weight of a primary action comes from the icon being the familiar one in the
familiar place, not from a block of colour — and a save drawn like the rest of the row cannot be
mistaken for a background that means something. The `x` that closes or abandons something is
neither green nor red, and stays plain.

A second convention follows from it: **the `x` is rightmost in every popup**, and the row ends
trashcan, save, `x`. The way out of an overlay is in the same corner wherever you are, so leaving
one is never something to look for — and that corner is worth more to the button reached for by
habit than to the one aimed at deliberately. The save keeps its familiar place beside it, which
is enough, because what makes it findable is the green disk and not the edge of the card.

## Markdown bodies

Every app keeps its text as markdown, so every app has to show it. **One renderer, in `shared/`,

